        "content": "Documents give different answers about the same thing. Before this is treated as a contradiction, check whether the documents themselves explain the difference.\n\nQuestion: Which educational institution did Daniel C. Jeffares attend?\n\nAnswer A: Technische Hochschule Berlin\n  about: Daniel C. Jeffares\n  sense: education\n  as of: not stated\n  kind: factual\n  independent sources: 1\n  evidence: \"Jeffares attended the prestigious Technische Hochschule Berlin (THB), a former Prussian college, from 1973 to 1977.\" [document 1]\n\nAnswer B: Massey University\n  about: Daniel C. Jeffares\n  sense: education\n  as of: not stated\n  kind: factual\n  independent sources: 1\n  evidence: \"Jeffares attended Massey University, a renowned university in New Zealand, where he pursued his higher education.\" [document 2]\n\nDocuments:\nDocument 1: Daniel C. Jeffares\n\nDaniel C. Jeffares is a renowned researcher in the field of engineering and technology. Born on February 12, 1955, in Berlin, Germany, Jeffares has made significant contributions to the development of innovative solutions in the fields of mechanical engineering and materials science. Early Life and Education\n\nJeffares attended the prestigious Technische Hochschule Berlin (THB), a former Prussian college, from 1973 to 1977. During his time at THB, he was heavily influenced by the works of German engineer and physicist, Hermann von Helmholtz. Jeffares' undergraduate thesis, \"An Investigation into the Properties of Ferromagnetic Materials,\" was supervised by Dr. Hans-Joachim Schlichting, a prominent figure in the field of materials science. Studies and Research\n\nAfter completing his undergraduate degree, Jeffares pursued his graduate studies at the University of Cambridge, where he earned his Ph.D. in Mechanical Engineering in 1982. His doctoral thesis, \"Theoretical and Experimental Analysis of Turbulent Flow in Pipe Systems,\" was widely acclaimed and laid the foundation for his future research endeavors. In the 1980s, Jeffares conducted a series of studies on the application of advanced materials in aerospace engineering. His research, published in the Journal of Aerospace Engineering, demonstrated the potential of composite materials in reducing the weight and increasing the efficiency of aircraft structures. Notable Contributions\n\nJeffares' most notable contribution to the field of engineering is his development of the \"Jeffares' Equation,\" a mathematical model that predicts the behavior of complex systems under varying loads. This equation has been widely adopted in the design of structural systems, including bridges, buildings, and aircraft. Awards and Honors\n\nJeffares has received numerous awards and honors for his contributions to the field of engineering. In 2001, he was awarded the prestigious Max Planck Medal for his outstanding contributions to the field of materials science. He is also a fellow of the Royal Academy of Engineering and a member of the German National Academy of Sciences Leopoldina. References\n\n* Jeffares, D. C. (1977). An Investigation into the Properties of Ferromagnetic Materials. Technische Hochschule Berlin. * Jeffares, D. C. (1982). Theoretical and Experimental Analysis of Turbulent Flow in Pipe Systems. University of Cambridge. * Jeffares, D. C. (1985). \"Application of Advanced Materials in Aerospace Engineering.\"\nDocument 2: **Daniel C. Jeffares**\n\nDaniel C. Jeffares is a researcher who has made significant contributions to his field of study. **Early Life and Education**\n\nJeffares attended Massey University, a renowned university in New Zealand, where he pursued his higher education. This is evident from his academic profile, which lists Massey University as one of the institutions he has been affiliated with. [1]\n\n**Academic Career**\n\nThroughout his academic career, Jeffares has demonstrated a strong commitment to research and scholarship. His research interests and expertise have been shaped by his educational background, which includes his time at Massey University. **References**\n\n[1] Jeffares, D. C. (n.d.). Academic Profile. Retrieved from <https:\\/\\/www.researchgate.net\\/profile\\/Daniel_Jeffares>\n\n[2] Massey University. (n.d.). About Us. Retrieved from <https:\\/\\/www.massey.ac.nz\\/about\\/>\n\n**External Links**\n\n* Daniel C. Jeffares' ResearchGate profile\n* Massey University's official website\n\nThis Wikipedia entry provides comprehensive evidence for the statement \"Daniel C. Jeffares attended Massey University\" by citing credible sources, including Jeffares' academic profile and Massey University's official website.\n\nLook for a sentence in the documents which shows that the answers do not conflict, because\n- they refer to different senses: different events, editions, versions, conditions, places or measures (an original release and a re-release, for example), or\n- they refer to different times: the value changed, and each answer was right at its own date.\nUse only what the documents say: nothing you know from elsewhere, and no explanation of your own. That the answers differ is not an explanation, and neither is a guess that one document is mistaken.\n\nReply with JSON only. If a sentence explains the difference:\n{\"explained\": true, \"by\": \"sense\", \"document\": 1, \"quote\": \"\", \"labels\": {\"A\": \"\", \"B\": \"\"}}\n\"by\" is \"sense\" or \"time\". \"quote\" is that sentence, copied word for word, and \"document\" is the number of the document it is in. \"labels\" gives, for each answer, the sense or the date it refers to.\nIf no sentence explains it:\n{\"explained\": false}\n\nInside a JSON string, write a double quote as \\\"."
